'''Shōgoi''' (正五位) is one of the ranks in the {{ill|Japanese|ja|日本|12=draft}} {{ill|Ritsuryō system|ja|律令制|12=draft}} and [[Shinkai (divine rank)|Shinkai (divine ranks)]]. It is positioned below {{ill|Junior Fourth Rank|ja|従四位|1=Junior Fourth Rank|12=draft}} and above {{ill|Junior Fifth Rank|ja|従五位|1=Junior Fifth Rank|12=draft}}.<ref>https://www.wakapoetry.net/background/society/court-offices-titles-and-ranks/court-ranks/</ref> Posthumous ranks for this level are referred to as ''Zōshōgoi''.

== Overview ==

Shōgoi, under the {{ill|Ritsuryō system|ja|律令制|12=draft}}, was divided into upper and lower grades. In terms of {{ill|official ranks|ja|官位|1=Kan'i system|12=draft}}, it corresponded primarily to {{ill|Kyōkan|ja|京官|12=draft}} (capitals officers), especially positions such as {{ill|Benkan|ja|弁官|12=draft}} ({{ill|Chūben|ja|左右中弁|12=draft}} or {{ill|Shōben|ja|少弁|12=draft}}) of the {{ill|Daijō-kan|ja|太政官|12=draft}}, {{ill|Daibu|ja|大輔|12=draft}} of the {{ill|Eight Ministries|ja|八省|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ōbanshi|ja|大判事|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ōzen-shoku|ja|大膳職|12=draft}} (Ōzen-daibu), {{ill|Shōshō|ja|少将|12=draft}} of the {{ill|Konoe Guards|ja|近衛府|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Ōkan|ja|衛門府|12=draft}}. These positions marked the middle-tier bureaucratic status.

In the {{ill|samurai|ja|武家|12=draft}} class, the {{ill|Hōjō clan|ja|北条氏|12=draft}} received this rank during the {{ill|Kamakura period|ja|鎌倉時代|12=draft}}, while it was granted to {{ill|daimyō|ja|大名|12=draft}} during the {{ill|Toyotomi regime|ja|豊臣氏|12=draft}}. However, during the {{ill|Edo period|ja|江戸時代|12=draft}}, appointments to Shōgoi decreased as focus shifted to {{ill|Jūshi'i|ja|従四位|1=Junior Fourth Rank|12=draft}} and {{ill|Jūgoi|ja|従五位|1=Junior Fifth Rank|12=draft}}. In this era, the rank was mainly awarded to {{ill|court officials|ja|宮廷|12=draft}} like {{ill|Chikashōnin|ja|地下官人|12=draft}} serving the {{ill|Imperial court|ja|朝廷|12=draft}}, {{ill|Shinnōke|ja|親王家|12=draft}}, {{ill|Gosekke|ja|五摂家|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Seiga families|ja|清華家|12=draft}}.

From the {{ill|Meiji period|ja|明治|12=draft}}, with the enactment of the {{ill|Rank Grant Ordinance|ja|叙位条例|12=draft}}, ranks above {{ill|Jūshi'i|ja|従四位|1=Junior Fourth Rank|12=draft}} were granted by imperial decree, while Shōgoi and below were conferred by submission (奏授). This rank often recognized mid-level samurai, {{ill|military officers|ja|軍人|12=draft}}, and {{ill|bureaucrats|ja|官僚|12=draft}}, especially those with notable contributions during the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|12=draft}}. Historical figures awarded the posthumous Shōgoi include {{ill|Mamiya Rinzō|ja|間宮林蔵|12=draft}} (granted in {{ill|1904|ja|1904th year|12=draft}}) and {{ill|Hōjō Sanetoki|ja|北条実時|12=draft}} (granted in {{ill|1915|ja|1915th year|12=draft}}) for cultural accomplishments like founding the {{ill|Kanazawa Library|ja|金沢文庫|12=draft}}.

Under the current system, ranks are conferred posthumously as honors. Recipients often include distinguished {{ill|university|ja|大学|12=draft}} and {{ill|college|ja|短期大学|12=draft}} professors, {{ill|business leaders|ja|経営者|12=draft}}, {{ill|civil servants|ja|公務員|12=draft}}, and notable {{ill|scholars|ja|学者|12=draft}}, {{ill|artists|ja|芸術家|12=draft}}, and {{ill|performers|ja|芸能人|12=draft}} who received awards like the {{ill|Order of the Rising Sun, Gold Rays with Neck Ribbon|ja|旭日中綬章|12=draft}}.

== Recipients of Posthumous Shōgoi (Zōshōgoi) After the Meiji Restoration ==
